/-- The statement of thm:corner(odd), modules/corner/corner.qpp:205: Let \(n\ge2\), \(k_0\ge2\), \(d\ge1\) and \(\delta\ge1\) be counts, \(N=k_0\delta\), \(\gamma=\gcd(k_0,d)\), \(\kappa=k_0/\gamma\) and \(\rho=d/\gamma\), and let \(f=h^{k_0}\varepsilon+g+f'\) be a polynomial in \(n\) variables with rational coefficients, where \(h\) and \(g\) are forms of degrees \(\delta\) and \(N+d\), \(\varepsilon\) is a polynomial with \(\varepsilon(0)=1\), and every term of \(f'\) has degree at least \(N+d+1\). Let \(\Sigma=\{h=0\}\) be smooth in the projective space of dimension \(n-1\), and let the scheme \(Z=\Sigma\cap\{g=0\}\), cut out by \(h\) and \(g\), be a smooth complete intersection of dimension \(n-3\), empty for \(n=2\): \(\{g=0\}\) meets \(\Sigma\) transversally, and \(dh\) and \(dg\) are independent at every point of \(Z\). Put \(N_S=\kappa(N+d)\), \(\nu_S=n\kappa+\rho\), \(s_S=-\nu_S/N_S\), \(\sigma_E=n+Ns\) and \(\sigma_S=\nu_S+N_Ss\); at \(s_S\) the costs \(\alpha_L=\gamma(n-\delta)/(N+d)\) and \(\alpha_R=1/\kappa\) of the two neighbours of the corner and the cost \(\alpha_V=1+s_S\) of each of its \(\gamma\) points on the strict transform tie, \(\alpha_L+\alpha_R+\gamma\alpha_V=\gamma\). A prime \(p\) is good if \(p\) does not divide \(\gamma\), the coefficients of \(h\), \(g\), \(\varepsilon\) and \(f'\) are \(p\)-integral, and the reductions of \(h\) and \(g\) modulo \(p\) keep the two hypotheses; all but finitely many primes are good. At a good prime write \(\#\) for the number of points over \(\mathbb F_p\) of a reduction, \(r(\xi)\) for the number of \(v\in\mathbb F_p\) with \(v^{\gamma}=-g(\xi)\) at a point \(\xi\) of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), which does not depend on the representative of \(\xi\) as \(\gamma\) divides \(N+d\), and is at most \(\gcd(\gamma,p-1)<p\), as \(-g(\xi)\) lies in the cyclic group \(\mathbb F_p^\times\) of order \(p-1\), and \(\#\tilde V=\sum_\xi r(\xi)\); with \(T=p^{-s}\) put \(X_E=p^{-n}T^N\), \(X_S=p^{-\nu_S}T^{N_S}\), \(F=(1-T)/(1-p^{-1}T)\), \(P_1=\sum_{b=1}^{\rho}p^{-na-b}T^{Na+k_0b}\) with \(a=\lfloor\kappa b/\rho\rfloor+1\), and \(P_2=\sum_{a=1}^{\kappa}p^{-na-b}T^{(N+d)a}\) with \(b=\lfloor\rho a/\kappa\rfloor+1\); and write \([P]\) for \(1\) when \(P\) holds and \(0\) otherwise. Then: (i) \(Z_{top,0}(s)=\frac{n-\chi(\Sigma)}{\sigma_E}+\frac{1}{\sigma_S}\big(\chi(\Sigma)\big(\kappa+\frac{\rho}{\sigma_E}\big)-\big(\gamma\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\kappa\chi(Z)\big)\frac{s}{1+s}\big)\) and, at a good prime, \(Z_0(s)=(1-p^{-1})\big(p^{1-n}\#(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus\Sigma)\frac{X_E}{1-X_E}+\frac{p^{2-n}}{1-X_S}\big(\#\Sigma\big((1-p^{-1})\frac{P_1}{1-X_E}+P_2+(1-p^{-1})X_S\big)-\#\tilde V\,p^{-1}X_SF-\#Z\,FP_2\big)\big)\); so the poles of \(Z_{top,0}\) lie in \(\{-n/N,s_S,-1\}\), and \(Z_0\) is a quotient of polynomials in \(T\) with rational coefficients whose denominator divides \((1-X_E)(1-X_S)(1-p^{-1}T)\); (ii) for \(\delta\ne n\) and \(s_S\ne-1\) the residue of \(Z_{top,0}\) at \(s_S\) is \(\big(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)A_\star+\chi(Z)A_Z\big)/N_S\), with \(A_\star=1/\alpha_L+1/\alpha_R+\gamma(1/\alpha_V-1)\) and \(A_Z=1/\alpha_L+1/(\alpha_R\alpha_V)\), and for \(N\ne n\) the residue at \(-n/N\) is \(n(\delta-n+\chi(\Sigma))/(N(\delta-n))\); for \(\delta>n\) put \(\beta=(\delta-n)/(N+d)\), so that \(\alpha_L=-\gamma\beta\) and \(\alpha_R+\gamma\alpha_V=\gamma(1+\beta)\) is a tie of positive masses; then \(-A_\star=(1-\alpha_V)(\alpha_V-\gamma\beta\alpha_R)/(\beta\alpha_R\alpha_V)\) and \(-A_Z=(\alpha_V-k_0\beta)/(\gamma\beta\alpha_V)\) are quotients of positive masses, \(A_Z-A_\star=(\kappa-\gamma)(1-\alpha_V)/\alpha_V\), the residue at \(s_S\) is not zero, of the sign \((-1)^{n+1}\) when \(\kappa\ge \gamma\), and, when \(\kappa<\gamma\), of the sign \((-1)^n\) for \(n\ge4\), positive for \(n=3\) and \(\gamma<2\kappa\), negative for \(n=3\) and \(\gamma\ge2\kappa\), and negative for \(n=2\); and the residue at \(-n/N\) is not zero; for \(\delta<n\) put \(D=N+d\), \(m=n-\delta\), \(V=N_S-\nu_S\), so that \(\alpha_V=V/N_S\), and the integer \(G=(k_0-1)D\chi(\Sigma)-(k_0-\gamma^2)m\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\); then for \(s_S\ne-1\), \(N_S\) times the residue at \(s_S\) is \(\nu_SG/(\gamma mV)\), zero exactly when \(G\) is and otherwise of the sign of \(G\) times that of \(1+s_S\); for \(n\ge4\), \(G\) has the sign \((-1)^{n+1}\) when \(\kappa>\gamma\) and \((-1)^n\) when \(\kappa<\gamma\), except at \((n,\delta,k_0,d)=(4,1,2,1)\), where \(G=0\), and for \(\kappa=\gamma\), \(G=(k_0-1)D\chi(\Sigma)\) is negative for odd \(n\) and \(\delta\ge3\) and positive otherwise; for \(n=3\), \(G>0\) when \(\gamma<2\kappa\), \(G<0\) when \(\gamma>2\kappa\), and for \(\gamma=2\kappa\), \(G=4\kappa(\kappa-\rho)\) at \(\delta=1\) and \(G=-2(k_0+d)\) at \(\delta=2\); for \(n=2\), \(G=(k_0-1)(D-1)+\gamma^2-1>0\) and \(s_S>-1\); so for \(\delta<n\) and \(s_S\ne-1\) the residue at \(s_S\) is not zero, except at \((4,1,2,1)\); for \(\gamma=1\), \(N_S\) times the residue at \(s_S\) is \(\frac{(1-\alpha_R)(1-\alpha_V)}{\alpha_L\alpha_R\alpha_V}\cdot\frac Q{N+d}\) with \(Q=(N+d+\delta-n)\chi(\Sigma)+(n-\delta)\chi(Z)\), and \(G=(k_0-1)Q\); so for \(\delta<n\) and \(n\ge3\) the number \(Q\) has the sign \((-1)^{n+1}\) except at \((4,1,2,1)\), where it is \(0\), and for \(d=1\) and \(n\ge3\) the residue at \(s_S\) away from \((4,1,2,1)\) has the sign \((-1)^{n+1}\) for \(n\le N\) and \((-1)^n\) for \(n>N\); at \(n=2\), where \(\delta=1\), \(Q=D-1\); (iii) at a good prime, for \(\delta\ne n\) and \(s_S\ne-1\), let \(t\) be the class of the variable in the field \(\mathbb Q[t]/(t^{N_S}-p)\), so that \(t^{N_S}=p\), and write \([k]=1+t+\dots+t^{k-1}\) for a count \(k\), with \([0]=0\), and \(L(\alpha)=(p-1)/(t^{\alpha N_S}-1)\) for a cost \(\alpha\ne0\) with \(\alpha N_S\) an integer; the value of \((1-X_S)Z_0\) at \(T=t^{\nu_S}\), the point \(s=s_S\), where \(X_S=1\), is \((1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\big(\sum_\xi\Lambda_{r(\xi)}+\#Z\,\Lambda_Z\big)\), the sum over the points \(\xi\) of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), where \(\Lambda_r=(p-1-r)+L(\alpha_L)+L(\alpha_R)+rL(\alpha_V)\) and \(\Lambda_Z=(p-1)+L(\alpha_L)+L(\alpha_R)L(\alpha_V)\); for \(\delta>n\) each \(-\Lambda_r\), \(0\le r\le \gamma\), and \(-\Lambda_Z\), and for \(\delta<n\) and \(s_S>-1\) each \(\Lambda_r\), \(0\le r\le \gamma\) and \(r<p\), as every \(r(\xi)\) is, and \(\Lambda_Z\), is a quotient of two sums of powers of \(t\) with nonnegative coefficients, neither zero, and no such sum is zero in the field; so in both cases the value is not zero, and \(s_S\) is a pole of \(Z_0\), exactly when \(\Sigma\) has a point over \(\mathbb F_p\), which holds at every large prime for \(n\ge3\); for \(\gamma=1\), with \(\langle\alpha\rangle=(t^{\alpha N_S}-1)/(p-1)\) for a cost \(\alpha\) with \(\alpha N_S\) whole, the value is \((1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\frac{\langle1-\alpha_R\rangle\langle1-\alpha_V\rangle}{\langle\alpha_L\rangle\langle\alpha_R\rangle\langle\alpha_V\rangle}\big(\langle1-\alpha_L\rangle\,\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\#Z\big)\), which for \(\delta>n\), with \(A=k_0(\delta-n)\), is \(-(1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}t^A\frac{[\nu_S][N_S-N-d]}{[A][N+d][N_S-\nu_S]}\big([N_S+A]\,\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)+[N_S]\,\#Z\big)\); for \(\alpha_L<1\) the value is \((-1)^{[\delta>n]+[s_S<-1]}\) times a quotient of two sums of powers of \(t\) with nonnegative coefficients, whose numerator is not zero exactly when \(\Sigma\) has a point over \(\mathbb F_p\); for \(\alpha_L=1\) it is \(-\#Z\) times such a quotient, with a numerator that is not zero; and for \(\alpha_L>1\) it is zero exactly when \(\Sigma\) has no point over \(\mathbb F_p\), if \(\alpha_L\) is not whole, and exactly when \((p^\ell-1)\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)=p^\ell(p-1)\#Z\), if \(\ell=\alpha_L-1\) is whole; (iv) for \(\delta=n\) the candidates \(-n/N\) and \(s_S\) meet at \(-1/k_0\), a pole of order two of \(Z_{top,0}\) with leading coefficient \(\rho\chi(\Sigma)/(NN_S)\) when \(n\ne3\), and a simple pole with residue \(1/k_0+3(\kappa-